From: stephan-xyz <noreply@redox-rpi>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] aarch64: broadcast the TLB flush before freeing frames

Flusher::flush frees the queued frames once enough CPUs acknowledged
the shootdown. A CPU that left the address space between the IPI and the
used_by check can still acknowledge, so the count can be reached before
every core that maps the address space flushed. On aarch64 the local
flush is a broadcast tlbi vmalle1is that has completed on all cores after
its dsb ish, so also do it when this CPU is not in the address space and
other CPUs are (redox-rpi #332).
---
--- a/src/context/memory.rs
+++ b/src/context/memory.rs
@@ -3046,6 +3046,13 @@
 
         if self.active_cpus.contains(current_cpu_id) {
             rmm::PageFlushAll::<RmmA>::new().flush();
+        } else if cfg!(target_arch = "aarch64") && affected_cpu_count > 0 {
+            // On aarch64 the flush is a broadcast `tlbi vmalle1is` that has
+            // completed on every core once its `dsb ish` returns. Do it here as
+            // well, so the frames queued below are never freed while another
+            // core can still reach them through its TLB, even if an
+            // acknowledgement meant for another flush arrives first.
+            rmm::PageFlushAll::<RmmA>::new().flush();
         }
 
         #[cfg(target_arch = "aarch64")]
